---
import {
  exampleBoard,
  exampleEvidence,
  exampleNext,
  examplePhases,
  exampleSpend,
  exampleStatusLine,
} from "../data/example-board.js";
import { installCommand, npmInstallCommand } from "../data/install.js";
import { url } from "../data/site.js";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const tasks = exampleBoard.tasks.map((task) => ({
  id: task.id,
  title: task.title,
  status: task.status,
  cost: exampleSpend(task.id),
}));
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Pi Maestro: orchestrated, reviewed work for Pi"
  description="Pi Maestro is a Pi extension. Your model plans a goal into tasks, fresh-context executors do them, and an independent reviewer approves each result."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>Plan once. Execute in fresh contexts. Approve after review.</h1>
        <p class="hero-lede">
          Pi Maestro is an extension for pi. The model in your session splits a goal into tasks with success criteria. Each task runs in its own pi process on a cheaper model, and a separate read-only reviewer has to approve it before it counts.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href={url("/docs/getting-started/")}>Install Pi Maestro</a>
          <a class="button button-quiet" href={url("/docs/")}>Read the docs</a>
        </div>
        <div class="install-command code-block">
          <button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy={installCommand} aria-label="Copy install command">Copy</button>
          <pre><code>{installCommand}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="hero-note">
          Installs from GitHub. For a published release, use <code>{npmInstallCommand}</code>. Requires pi 1.5 or newer and Node.js 22.19 or newer. MIT licensed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="board-example">
        <figcaption>Example board mid-run: T2 was rejected once and is on its second attempt. Not live data.</figcaption>
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Four tasks on a Maestro board with their status and spend</caption>
          <thead class="board-head"><tr><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col">Title</th><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Spend</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {tasks.map((task) => (
              <tr>
                <th class="task-id" scope="row">{task.id}</th>
                <td>{task.title}</td>
                <td><span class={`status status-${task.status}`}>{task.status}</span></td>
                <td>{task.cost}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="board-footer"><code>{exampleStatusLine}</code></p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="first-run" aria-labelledby="first-run-title">
    <div class="shell first-run">
      <div class="first-run-steps">
        <h2 id="first-run-title">What a first run shows you</h2>
        <p>Run these in a Git repository that has at least one commit. Nothing below needs configuration; the default preset uses your pi model.</p>
        <ol class="step-list">
          <li>
            <p>Install from your shell, then start pi in the repository. Inside pi, <code>/maestro doctor</code> checks config, authenticated models, Git, and worktrees without printing keys.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy={installCommand} aria-label="Copy shell install command">Copy</button>
              <pre><code>{installCommand}</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Hand over a goal. Your model turns it into tasks with success criteria. With <code>planGate</code> on, <code>/maestro plan</code> shows them for approval first.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy="/maestro start migrate the auth module to the new session API and add tests" aria-label="Copy start command">Copy</button>
              <pre><code>/maestro start migrate the auth module to the new session API and add tests</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Run the loop, then read the board. <code>/maestro board</code> opens on the current phase and never calls a model.</p>
            <div class="code-block">
              <button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy={"/maestro drive\n/maestro board"} aria-label="Copy drive and board commands">Copy</button>
              <pre><code>/maestro drive
/maestro board</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <figure class="board-demo">
        <figcaption>
          The dashboard for the example board above. Phase names, state labels, evidence lines, and <code>Next:</code> text are the ones <code>/maestro board</code> prints; the task data is made up.
        </figcaption>
        <section aria-labelledby="demo-phase">
          <h3 id="demo-phase">Phase</h3>
          <ol class="phase-rail">
            {examplePhases.map((phase) => (
              <li class:list={{ "is-current": phase.state === "current" }} aria-current={phase.state === "current" ? "step" : undefined}>
                <span class="phase-name">{phase.label}</span>
                <span class="phase-state">{phase.state}</span>
                <span class="phase-tasks">{phase.tasks}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </section>
        <section aria-labelledby="demo-evidence">
          <h3 id="demo-evidence">Review evidence</h3>
          <p class="demo-path"><code>{exampleEvidence.path}</code></p>
          <ul class="evidence-lines">
            {exampleEvidence.lines.map((line) => <li><code>{line}</code></li>)}
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section aria-labelledby="demo-next">
          <h3 id="demo-next">Your next action</h3>
          <p class="next-line"><span>While the retry runs</span><code>Next: {exampleNext.live}</code></p>
          <p class="next-line"><span>If the drive stops first</span><code>Next: {exampleNext.stopped}</code></p>
        </section>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2>From goal to approved work</h2>
        <p>Run <code>/maestro start &lt;goal&gt;</code>. Everything after that is recorded on a board in <code>.pi/maestro/</code>, so a restart loses nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="flow">
        <li>
          <h3>Plan</h3>
          <p>Each task gets a self-contained brief, 1 to 12 success criteria, bounded write paths, dependencies, and a tier. With <code>planGate</code> on, nothing runs until you approve the plan.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Execute</h3>
          <p>Tasks whose dependencies are approved run in parallel, three at a time by default, as separate <code>pi --mode rpc</code> processes. Each uses the model and fallbacks of its tier and sees only its brief and the reports of approved dependencies.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Review</h3>
          <p>A reviewer with a fresh context and <code>read,grep,find,ls</code> checks every criterion and ends with <code>VERDICT: APPROVE</code> or <code>VERDICT: REQUEST_CHANGES</code>. A rejection sends the findings into the next attempt.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Integrate</h3>
          <p>Artifact checks and any trusted verification command run before the work lands as one conventional commit per task.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <h2>The orchestrator stays small</h2>
        <p>The expensive model plans, picks tiers, and decides what to do when a drive stops. It never reads raw executor output. A single <code>maestro_drive</code> call spans the whole run and returns one bounded outcome, so the orchestrator spends one turn on a drive instead of polling it.</p>
        <p>The model has three tools: <code>maestro_plan</code>, <code>maestro_update</code>, and <code>maestro_drive</code>. Approval, pause, abort, and recovery are commands only you can run.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Spend and attempts are capped</h2>
        <p>By default one attempt is cut off at $5, and no new batch starts once the board's spend reaches $25. A task gets three attempts, and a drive runs at most 20 rounds. When a limit trips, the drive stops with a named reason and the safe next step.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={url("/docs/operations/")}>What each stop means</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <h2>Everything is inspectable</h2>
        <p>The board, a status history, raw event logs, executor and reviewer sessions, and checkpoint branches stay on disk. <code>/maestro board</code> walks eight phases from discovery to completion. <code>/maestro timeline</code>, <code>costs</code>, and <code>reconcile</code> read that evidence without calling a model.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={url("/docs/commands/")}>Command reference</a></p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>What it does not do</h2>
        <p>Maestro is not a sandbox. Executors edit files with your account's permissions, and verification commands are arbitrary local code that only your user config can define. Reviewers are read-only by default, and manual approval skips them. An untrusted project cannot change budgets, models, or tool lists.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={url("/docs/security/")}>Security and trust boundaries</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section install-band">
    <div class="shell install-layout">
      <div>
        <h2>Install from Git or use a pinned commit</h2>
        <p>The GitHub install tracks the default branch, and <code>pi update</code> pulls newer commits. Add <code>@&lt;ref&gt;</code> to the source to pin a commit. A local checkout needs its dependencies installed first. <a class="text-link" href={url("/docs/getting-started/")}>Every install option</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="code-block">
        <button class="copy-button" type="button" data-copy={installCommand} aria-label="Copy install command">Copy</button>
        <pre><code>{installCommand}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
